    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The novel retells the real journey of the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 629, as a fantasy in which demons, immortals and a monkey who rebelled against Heaven escort a pilgrim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li><li>Its stories circulated in oral storytelling for centuries before the 100-chapter form of the 16th century, and its authorship is still disputed, though Wu Cheng'en is traditionally named.</li><li>It has been read as a comic adventure, a satire of Chinese bureaucracy, a source of spiritual reflection and an allegory, and it reached English readers through Arthur Waley's abridgement of 1942.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sun Wukong's staff weighs 17,550 pounds (7,960 kg) in the novel. It began as a pillar supporting the undersea palace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-gyalpo">Dragon King</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">East Sea</a>, and he can shrink it to the size of a needle and keep it in his ear.</p></aside>
    </section>

